        kind: 'faded',
        text: 'A distribution centre runs 40 identical conveyor gearboxes (boxes of gears that turn fast motor speed into slow belt speed). Over three years 14 failed.\n\nThe reliability engineer fitted one Weibull on all 14 failures and got beta = 1.1. His conclusion: “Random failures, so no preventive task makes sense. Run to failure.”\n\nIn the work orders you see two kinds of failure: bearing failures, mostly within four months of a gearbox rebuild (a full overhaul), and gear-tooth wear on units older than six years. The 26 gearboxes that never failed are not in the analysis.',
        ask: 'What would you do next?',
        reveal: 'Challenge the fit before accepting “run to failure”. Split the data per failure mode and add the 26 gearboxes that never failed as suspensions. The bearing failures soon after a rebuild point to infant mortality (beta below 1): look at the rebuild itself, such as alignment, bearing fitting, lubricant type and quantity, and the start-up check.\n\nThe gear wear on old units points to wear-out (beta above 1): oil analysis (testing the oil for metal particles) or vibration monitoring, or planned replacement around the age where wear starts. One fit on mixed modes gave a beta near 1 that described neither problem, and would have led to doing nothing about two fixable ones.',
