        text: 'You’re in an RCM workshop on a cooling-water system at a chemical plant. Pump A runs; standby pump B starts automatically when pressure drops.\n\nThe current PM plan has a monthly visual inspection of pump B: “no leaks, no noise, looks fine”. In eight years nobody has tested whether pump B actually starts when pressure drops. The team is about to mark pump B as “covered”.',
        ask: 'What would you do next?',
        reveal: 'Stop the team marking it covered. Pump B’s failure to start is a hidden failure: in normal running nobody notices, and you find out on the day pump A trips (shuts down on a fault). A visual check sees leaks, not whether the pressure switch, the controls and the motor start the pump.\n\nThe plan needs a failure-finding task: a scheduled test that drops the pressure or simulates a trip and checks that pump B starts and delivers flow. Set the interval from how reliable the standby is and how much unavailability the plant can accept, for example with interval = 2 × MTBF × tolerated unavailability. Then ask the same question of every other protective device in the system.',
